2026-09-27T07:22:15.5002204Z Disclaimer: there's no guarantee of timely updates in case of security problems or other issues. If you want to use them in a security-or-otherwise-critical environment (say, on a production server), you do so at your own risk.
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5003753Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5004178Z Update Note
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5004537Z ===========
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5005033Z Please use this repository instead of ppa:fkrull/deadsnakes.
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5005501Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5005666Z Reporting Issues
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5006026Z ================
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5006221Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5006483Z Issues can be reported in the master issue tracker at:
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5007060Z https://github.com/deadsnakes/issues/issues
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5007432Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5007612Z Supported Ubuntu and Python Versions
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5008086Z ====================================
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5008381Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5008603Z - Ubuntu 22.04 (jammy) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.10)
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5009588Z - Ubuntu 24.04 (noble) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.12)
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5010228Z - Ubuntu 26.04 (resolute) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.14)
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5011424Z - Note: Python 3.10 (jammy), Python3.12 (noble), Python3.14 (resolute) are not provided by deadsnakes as upstream ubuntu provides those packages.
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5012366Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5012546Z Why some packages aren't built:
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5013286Z - Note: for 22.04+, older python versions requre libssl<3 so they are not currently built
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5014230Z - If you need these, reach out to asottile to set up a private ppa
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5014715Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5015254Z The packages may also work on other versions of Ubuntu or Debian, but that is not tested or supported.
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5015931Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5016080Z Packages
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5016493Z ========
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5016680Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5019742Z The packages provided here are loosely based on the debian upstream packages with some modifications to make them more usable as non-default pythons and on ubuntu.  As such, the packages follow debian's patterns and often do not include a full python distribution with just `apt install python#.#`.  Here is a list of packages that may be useful along with the default install:
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5022223Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5022687Z - `python#.#-dev`: includes development headers for building C extensions
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5023475Z - `python#.#-venv`: provides the standard library `venv` module
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5024722Z - `python#.#-distutils`: provides the standard library `distutils` module
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5025743Z - `python#.#-lib2to3`: provides the `2to3-#.#` utility as well as the standard library `lib2to3` module
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5110832Z - `python#.#-gdbm`: provides the standard library `dbm.gnu` module
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5111669Z - `python#.#-tk`: provides the standard library `tkinter` module
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5112156Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5112828Z Third-Party Python Modules
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5113232Z ==========================
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5113448Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5115108Z Python modules in the official Ubuntu repositories are packaged to work with the Python interpreters from the official repositories. Accordingly, they generally won't work with the Python interpreters from this PPA. As an exception, pure-Python modules for Python 3 will work, but any compiled extension modules won't.
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5116895Z 
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5117902Z To install 3rd-party Python modules, you should use the common Python packaging tools.  For an introduction into the Python packaging ecosystem and its tools, refer to the Python Packaging User Guide:
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5119190Z https://packaging.python.org/installing/
2026-09-27T07:22:15.5120088Z 
